I have a list of names and I want to sort them alphabetically.
I want to sort them in a case-sensitive way.
So, for example, "John" should come before "john".
I have tried the following code:

<code>names.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, undefined, {
  sensitivity: 'base'
}));
</code>

But it doesn't work.
I want to sort them in a case-sensitive way.
So, for example, "John" should come before "john".
I have tried the following code:

<code>names.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, undefined, {
  sensitivity: 'base'
}));
</code>

But it doesn't work.

Answer:

The `sensitivity: 'base'` option actually makes the comparison case-insensitive, which is the opposite of what you want. To achieve case-sensitive sorting, you should use the default behavior or explicitly set `sensitivity: 'accent'` or `sensitivity: 'case'`.

Here's the correct way to sort names in a case-sensitive manner:

<code>names.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, undefined, {
  sensitivity: 'case'
}));
</code>

This will ensure that uppercase letters come before lowercase letters, so "John" will be sorted

